The ballroom inside The Lodge at Lake Barrington Shores was filled to capacity for the annual Veterans Day ceremony, which was hosted by The Veterans of LBS.
Tom Gilder opened the ceremony with a welcome and recognition of local dignitaries. Karen Daulton Lange, village administrator of Lake Barrington, and state Rep. Nick Sauer, R-Lake Barrington, were in the audience.
Members of the Veterans of LBS presented the flags representing the different branches of the U.S. military. The audience stood for the Pledge of Allegiance, followed by Dave Raclaw's invocation.
A moment of silence at 11:11 a.m. honored all veterans.
The guest speaker was Corey Maples, chief warrant officer with the U.S. Coast Guard. He spoke on the history and future of the Coast Guard.
He said it is a multi-mission military force, including maritime safety, security and stewardship. It is the smallest of our service branches.
He said the future of the Coast Guard is in cybersecurity and the Arctic. Maples said Russia and China are already looking at the Arctic region for resources, so the Coast Guard is looking to serve in the Arctic, as well.
As always during the annual ceremony, a microphone was passed among audience members who shared remembrances of veterans.
A red, white and blue floral wreath was presented to honor recently deceased LBS veterans. Laura Jessen represented her father, Bob, while I represented my husband, Jim. Tom Schofield also was remembered.
Tom Gilder also thanked Diane Geiser and the "9 and Dine" golf group for raising $1,000 for the Veterans of LBS.
