Everyone looks forward to the warm summer sun and the outdoor activities that accompany it. While ultraviolet radiation from the sun provides us with many benefits, there are important risks with overexposure to be aware of.
Ultraviolet radiation reaches us in the form of UVA and UVB rays. Both rays can lead to skin cancer, however UVA rays penetrate the skin more deeply and are associated with skin damage and aging (wrinkling, leathering, sagging). UVB radiation, on the other hand, plays a key role in sunburn.
Clothing is our first line of defence against the sun��s harmful UV rays. The tightness of the weave, weight, fibre, colour and amount of skin covered all affect the protection they provide. In general, dark-coloured and more tightly woven fabrics are more protective. Hold your clothes up to the light and if you can see through them, UV radiation can penetrate them — and your skin.
Your next line of defence is sunscreen. Sun Protection Factor (SPF) is a measure of a sunscreen��s ability to prevent UVB from damaging the skin. SPF 15 filters out approximately 93 per cent of all incoming UVB rays. SPF 30 keeps out 97 per cent and SPF 50 keeps out 98 per cent. The difference may seem negligible, but for light-sensitive individuals, or those with a history of skin cancer, that extra protection will make a difference.
Sunscreen consists of chemical ingredients such as avobenzone and benzophenone that work by absorbing UV rays and reducing their penetration into the skin. Physical ingredients such as titanium dioxide and zinc oxide stay on top of the skin and deflect UV rays.
We recommend checking the label to understand what you are applying to your skin. Physical blockers are less likely to cause skin irritation and are better for those with skin allergies or sensitive skin. Some sunscreens include oxybenzone and octinoxate, which can damage coral reefs and disrupt the development of fish and wildlife. As such, Hawaii has put a ban on these ingredients, so take note if you are travelling.
Top 10 sunscreen Do��s & Don��ts
1. Do choose an SPF between 30 and 50.
2. Do use enough. You need to apply one teaspoon per area of the body to get the full benefit of your SPF. Depending on your body size, that means about one teaspoon for your face, head, and neck; one for each arm; one for each leg; one for your chest and abdomen, and one for your back. That��s seven teaspoons, or a little more than an ounce. Most people apply less than half that, which means the actual SPF they have on their body is lower than advertised.
3. Do allow ingredients to fully bind to the skin; apply 15 minutes before sun exposure.
4. Do choose a water-resistant sunscreen that will stay put when you sweat on hot days or while playing sports.
5. Don��t forget to reapply every two hours to be effective and immediately after swimming, toweling off or sweating during sports.
6. Don��t neglect other sun-protective behaviours, such as limiting time outside, seeking the shade and wearing sun-protective clothing.
7. Don��t expose children under the age of six months to the sun or sunscreen. Shade and protective clothing are the best ways to protect your{g} skin{g}.{r}
