Nestled in the north of Spain is the autonomous community known as Basque Country. Like every region around the world, Basque has cuisine that is all its own. Basque cuisine is made up of different types of meat and fish that is grilled over hot coals or stewed, dishes made with beans, tapas and sheep��s cheese. Olive oil, tomatoes and peppers are used a lot in preparing these dishes as is the case with other Mediterranean fare.
Typical Dishes From Basque Country
Being close to the sea, seafood ia a big part of food from this region of Spain. Some of the popular seafood dishes are salt cod, young eel, spider crab, cheeks of hake and baby squid. Others are gooseneck barnacles, grilled and roasted beef, chicken, lamb and wood pigeon. Sausages and cured ham are also popular as are cheese, fruits and vegetables including artichokes, asparagus and beans. Meals are often taken with sparkling wine, a mix of wine and cola, punch cocktail or Basque cider.
Basque Food San Francisco Restaurants
Basque cuisine has continued to grow in popularity as international cuisine. It is particularly popular in neighboring France. In 1991 the cuisine came into the U.S. when a restaurant was opened in Bronxville, NY, and later in Michigan. The cuisine then entered the San Francisco food scene when a number of restaurants specializing in Basque fare were opened in different parts of the city.
Apart from being a great place to hold an event, the Basque Cultural Center on 599 Railroad Avenue in South San Francisco is also a great place to sample authentic Basque cuisine. A popular choice with patrons is the family style dinner that has a bit of everything and is said to be good value for the money because of the variety and portions. The platter has a bit of veal, some sea bass, sweet bread, prawn, racks of lamb and pork chops. This is also a good place to go out in groups. Different kinds of soups, salads and desserts like spumoni, a dessert that combines chocolate, pistachio and strawberry ice cream are all to be enjoyed. Both American wine and beer as well as Basque beverages like cider are available.
