One of Australia's highest profile family feuds is due to go to open court today as two of Gina Rinehart's children seek to examine her conduct as head of a trust.
A New South Wales Supreme Court civil trial will examine allegations Mrs Rinehart engaged in serious misconduct over a trust set up by her father Lang Hancock.
Her children John Hancock and Bianca Rinehart accuse her of changing the date they could access their money without telling them.
Last week Mrs Rinehart's youngest daughter Ginia's appeal to have the feud dealt with in private arbitration was dismissed.
The 25-year-old argued the trial will be a ''farce''.
The appeal followed a surprise declaration by Gina Rinehart that she wants to be discharged as trustee and would like to go to mediation to appoint someone else.
The court heard Mrs Rinehart wants a family member with knowledge of the resources industry and no history of "wanting personal favours".
Her lawyer Bruce McClintock has said that should mean the matter is over.
"Mrs Rinehart has the power to appoint a new trustee or trustees - under the Western Australian Trustees Act, the documents governing the Trust and the agreement of the beneficiaries under the Hope Downs Deed - and is willing to do so," he said.
"But (she) first wishes to do so in consultation with the beneficiaries or their representatives.
"In her 20 years as trustee, Mrs Rinehart's devotion to building the family company HPPL has seen it through{r} many{r} difficult{g} times{r}."{r}
