It was roughly 3:30 p.m., shadows cascading over Coors Field on Sunday, when the bullpen phone rang. The 42,032 paying spectators had no way of knowing that it would be the most important call of the season. And thankfully, after Saturday night, it didn��t involve an umpire.
��Bullpen coach Jimmy (Wright) asked me if I could go two innings,�� recalled closer Huston Street. ��I said, ��Absolutely.�� It was a rush.��
There are no must-win games, but manager Jim Tracy wasn��t about to lose this one. He turned to Street. The closer recorded the save, but Clint Barmes was unofficially credited with the rescue in a 4-3 victory over the St. Louis Cardinals.
Just call it ��The Catch and Release.�� Barmes made an over-the-shoulder, tumbling grab and fired to first for a game-ending double play. It��s a wonder he didn��t fall back over as the crowd exhaled in unison.
Widespread panic can wait. The Rockies sit 2 1/2 games ahead of the scalding-hot Braves in the wild-card chase, while reducing their magic number to five with six games remaining.
��Greatest. Catch. Ever,�� said outfielder Carlos Gonzalez, only slightly exaggerating. ��All I could do was scream.��
For three days these two bullies traded slugs and words. The Cardinals accused the Rockies of doctoring their bullpen mound. The Rockies suggested St. Louis was playing head games. So, it came down to this between the increasingly irritated antagonists.
Runners were on first and third in the ninth inning. There was one out, even if tainted by controversy. Troy Tulowitzki made a remarkable, off-balance throw from the hole to retire a sliding Skip Schumaker at first base. Replays showed he was safe. Cardinals manager Tony La Russa, whose club benefited from a wide strike zone Saturday, argued briefly.
He��s always seeking an explanation and an edge. Friday, he told the plate umpire to cut Street��s warm-up pitches short because he took extra in the bullpen before entering the game. So rather than get the standard eight, he received three.
��That just made me want to beat them even more,�� Street said.
To secure Sunday��s victory, Street would need help. Tulo-witzki��s play allowed Street to intentionally walk Albert Pujols, but Street still had to deal with Ryan Ludwick. On a 1-2 slider, Ludwick muscled the ball into shallow right field.
��I knew I had no chance because we were playing deep to prevent any doubles,�� right fielder Ryan Spilborghs said.
Barmes made a great read, racing back to make an acrobatic catch. He managed to secure the ball as he slammed into the ground.
��Jaw-dropping,�� said pitcher Aaron Cook.
Spilborghs yelled for Barmes to throw to first base. Pujols gambled that the ball would land, leaving him at third with one out. That left him with no chance of getting back when Barmes fired to Todd Helton, setting off a wild celebration in the outfield.
��It��s the best play that anyone has ever made behind me,�� Street said after his first six-out save since Aug. 2, 2006. ��That��s why you see guys hugging Barmes and screaming. It��s been an emotional year. We want it so badly, and we believe so strongly in each other that we are going to get it done.��
