Former Pennsylvania Gov. and U.S. Attorney General Dick Thornburgh confirmed today he is not a part of the legal team that will argue to overturn NCAA sanctions against the Penn State football program.
But Thornburgh, as a hired reviewer of former FBI Director Louis J. Freeh's report on the Jerry Sandusky child sexual abuse scandal, certainly contributed to construction of the case.
Thornburgh produced a report for the late Joe Paterno's family in February that asserted many of Freeh's conclusions about a cover-up at Penn State - especially to the degree they implicated the legendary head football coach - are not conclusively supported by evidence.
The plaintiffs in the new civil case, including the late head coach's estate, are using that opinion to argue, in part, that the NCAA inappropriately used Freeh's report as the foundation for its unprecedented disciplinary actions against Penn State.
Thornburgh, the governor of Pennsylvania from 1979 through 1986, took some fresh questions about his work for the Paternos via e-mail today while travelling to Harrisburg to celebrate the 30th anniversary of a state economic development program.
Thornburgh said he is not involved as counsel in the latest NCAA suit and so declined to comment when asked for his take of its strengths and weaknesses.
But he did appear to leave the door open to being called as a witness, if necessary, on his Freeh Report analysis, and expressed some pleasure at having helped to save Joe Paterno's reputation from what he has termed a "rush to injustice."
"Those who have actually read our report find it to be a credible critique of the Freeh report," Thornburgh wrote in response to a question about the reaction he gets for his plunge in to the Penn State case.
"Many persons.... most prominently Phil Knight of NIKE and Bob Costas (who gave the Paternos the broadcast venue on which they rolled out their suit last week) have substantially altered their view of the Freeh report based upon our report," Thornburgh stated.
Other sources, most notably former state Attorney General Linda Kelly's investigators, have said there is evidence pointing toward a Sandusky cover-up, echoing and building on some of Freeh's findings.
Kelly's office filed charges late last year, and months after Joe Paterno's January 2012 death, against former Penn State President Graham Spanier and two of his senior administrators.
