THE REAGAN administration should be congratulated for offering its first comprehensive environmental legislation in four years. Unfortunately, the new $5.3 billion Superfund proposal will not be adequate to clean up the nation's worst hazardous waste sites.
In the last two years, the federal government has pumped $1.6 billion into hazardous waste cleanup programs. But the Superfund was stymied under the controversial leadership of Rita Lavelle, and few major cleanups were completed in the program's first years.
Because of the logjam, Florida environmental officials expect 150 to 250 Superfund sites to become eligible for cleanup funds at approximately the same time. If cleanup estimates around the nation coincide with Florida's estimates of a low of $2 million to a high of $150 million per site, the competition among states will be fierce and there will not be enough money for the nation's 786 Superfund sites.
The National Governor's Association calls the $5.3 billion to be spent over the next five years "totally inadequate" because it is less than the minimum of $8 billion that the Reagan administration previously has said would be necessary to clean up the nation's worst pollution sites.
The proposal unveiled by new Environmental Protection Agency Administrator Lee Thomas also would strike a devastating blow to states -- such as Florida -- that already have accepted a large share of cleanup responsibilities. The Superfund bill would double to 20 percent state contributions to Superfund projects.
By increasing the states' financial burden, the federal government would be amending its prior stand that the worst hazardous waste sites -- approximately 10 percent of the total -- are a national responsibility.
In numbers alone, states have assumed a far greater responsibility than the federal government by assuming cleanup duties for 90 percent of all hazardous waste sites. The federal perception that states are not doing enough is generally wrong.
Florida has 402 hazardous waste sites, and only 35 of those are on the Superfund list. EPA has given the state $6 million for Superfund sites, while the state has spent or budgeted $14.5 million for all hazardous waste sites. If EPA requires more state contributions to Superfund, cleanup of smaller sites -- that may pose as great a danger to public health as larger sites -- will have to be delayed.
The proposed funding mechanism, however, has good and bad sides. Currently, 13 percent of the Superfund comes out of the general federal budget and the remaining 87 percent from a tax on raw materials that are the sources of toxic wastes.
The proposal properly calls for the direct burden to the taxpayer to be dropped, and for the full financial burden to be carried by the petro-chemical industry and its customers. It is essentially a user tax that will not place an additional burden on the federal budget or national debt.
That proposed funding change is good and may encourage industries to be more zealous in proper handling of hazardous materials, but another proposed switch in funding sources is dangerous because of its questionable reliability.
