There are calls for a Queensland doctor to be stripped of his right to practise medicine and investigated over allegations he prematurely ended the lives of patients under his care.
Former Queensland Medical Board investigator Jo Barber says the doctor's actions are so dangerous he could have been charged with manslaughter or murder.
Ms Barber says there are a number of deaths linked to the doctor, who, after fronting the state's medical board, was allowed to continue practising as long as he was not working in intensive care.
One grieving family says they want the doctor stripped of the right to practice medicine after their 83-year-old mother was given a breathtaking ultimatum over the continuation of her life support.
The opera-loving grandmother was in intensive care and on a ventilator due to a lung condition.
Her children, who cannot be named for legal reasons, had no idea how quickly things had deteriorated but say she was "very able to understand... and knew what was ahead of her but she certainly wasn't prepared for what happened."
They say the doctor stood matter-of-factly at the end of the bed and delivered a brutally frank assessment.
"[He] said to her these are your options: we can either turn your respirator off, in which case you will die straight away. We can turn it back to room pressure, in which case you will go into a coma and then you will die. Or we can leave you as you are but your organs are breaking down and you've probably got about three or four days and I'll leave you with your family," said a male relative.
"And we're there at the side of the bed. Mum's eyes were just telling the story; she was in absolute horror."
The doctor then turned and walked away.
According to Ms Barber, who was the investigator for the medical board at the time, neither the woman nor the family ever got to choose one of those options.
That same night the doctor turned down her supply of oxygen. Sometime later a nurse discovered her in a distressed state and turned it back up, but it was too late.
"She was gasping for breath and I was told that her death was quite painful," Ms Barber said.
"In fact the nurses were so traumatised by nursing her to her death in the manner that she died that they bought psychologists in to treat them, the hospital did afterwards."
The medical board was alerted to the case, not by the family but by a fellow doctor who also alleged there were other deaths linked to the doctor in question.
"I was so alarmed I actually got up from my desk at that time - at the moment I read the file - and went straight to my manager and said: 'My God, you know this case is, it's incredible'," Ms Barber said.
"You've got a colleague, a medical professional documenting case after case of where a doctor has ended the lives of others. We need to immediately suspend this doctor and we need to refer this file to the police. This is murder and manslaughter."
'Utterly dysfunctional'
In the end, the state's medical board, two-thirds of which are doctors, allowed the doctor to retain his registration to practise with one condition: he stop working in intensive care.
Ms Barber, who revealed a series of concerns to 7.30 in April about malpractice in Queensland hospitals, says it is a case of "Caesar judging Caesar".
She says the review of such cases should be handled by a panel of people who are "legally minded", with the assistance of medical administrators.
"It's completely and utterly dysfunctional and if you were to look in the last 10 years, those [doctors] that actually had been struck off or completely gotten rid of as a result of their incompetence would be - you could count them on one hand. Maybe five or six," she said.
